  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCalculate $P({X}^{2}=3).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's first add an ${X}^{2}$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $9$ | $3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $9$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third rows of our table, we can immediately conclude that \n\n$$\nP({X}^{2}=3)=0.1+0.3=0.4.\n$$",
